task: gate-starved-suites
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/gate-starved-suites
worktree: ../okay-wt-starved

why: the two boxes still open under BACKLOG's `gate-honesty`. They are
  the tax every lane pays: the gate cried wolf four times in one day,
  and the real cost ran the other way — that session nearly dismissed
  a GENUINE defect (actor-stop-drain, 219 of 300 messages lost) as
  load, by analogy with these.

    suite                          in the gate   alone
    okay.kafka.TestKafkaInterop        237 s     0.084 s
    okay.intent.TestCutStops            39 s     0.059 s
    okay.ui TestSwing (the law)      timeout     passes
    okay.persist.TestStable      BindException   0.007 s

WHAT I HAVE READ ALREADY, before claiming, because the entry insists
each needs its OWN reading of whether its assertion depends on timing:

  - TestCutStops asserts TOKEN COUNTS and emission counts. No clock,
    no deadline, nothing timing-dependent.
  - TestKafkaInterop is mocks (`MockConsumer`), asserting values and
    their order. No clock either.
  - TestSwing's law renders twelve frames and compares each against a
    freshly built one. No clock.
  - TestStable's second test asserts `currentTerm == 5` and
    `votedFor == Some("1")`. It is not about networking at all; the
    port is incidental machinery, and it is bound the textbook wrong
    way:

        val port = { val ss = ServerSocket(0); val p = ss.getLocalPort; ss.close(); p }
        val node = RaftWire.Node("0", port, ...)

    ask the OS, CLOSE it, then race a neighbour to bind it again.
    `RaftWire.Node` passes its port straight to `ServerSocket(port)`,
    which takes 0 and lets the OS assign — so the pre-bind buys
    nothing and costs a collision.

So on the face of it all four say "the assertion does not depend on
timing", and AGENTS' first option (a longer deadline) is the honest
one for the three, with the port fixed properly for the fourth.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - 237 s against 0.084 s is 2 800x. CPU starvation on a 14-core box
    carrying six sibling builds does not multiply by 2 800. If that
    suite is SPINNING rather than starved — a poll or retry loop with
    no backoff, doing orders of magnitude more work under contention
    — then a longer deadline HIDES A DEFECT, and hiding one is
    exactly what this backlog entry exists to stop. The same question
    must be asked of the 39 s.
  - a longer deadline must be justified per suite and never swept:
    the entry says so, and a blanket `munitTimeout` across the repo
    would be the sweeping it forbids.
  - "it passes alone" proves nothing about the fix. The mechanism has
    to be reproduced, and this session already learned that a load
    harness may FAIL to reproduce it (28 spinners did not reproduce
    the ui pair) — in which case isolate the variable instead.

predict: kafka is NOT plain starvation. The ratio is too large, and I
  expect a busy poll loop — `pollMillis = 10` with a mock that
  answers instantly — that makes progress only when it wins a
  timeslice. TestCutStops and TestSwing I expect to be genuinely
  starved, deadline-only. Recorded so being wrong shows.

--- FOUND, 2026-09-09 ---

THE PREDICTION WAS RIGHT ABOUT KAFKA AND WRONG ABOUT WHY. It is not
a spin, and it is not starvation: the 237 s was never that suite's.

    479c510b gate-hygiene (a sibling lane, 12:19 today):
    "TestRepoAgent indexes the whole repository and ran 203-237 s
     under four sbts against its 120 s budget, green alone — tagged
     Live"

Same number, same gate, different suite. The entry's table named
`okay.kafka.TestKafkaInterop` for a time that belonged to
`okay.demo.TestRepoAgent`, and the intent row looks like the same
error: gate-hygiene measured `TestOfflineGate` and `TestTypoRobustness`
at "4 s alone, 48-61 s under load" and gave them a 180 s deadline,
while my row named `TestCutStops` at 39 s. (The kafka attribution is
certain — the number matches exactly. The intent one is likely rather
than proven: different numbers, same module, same gate.)

MEASURED, rather than argued. A full gate under 28 CPU burners on a
box already at load 20 — load average 101 at the end, three times the
"four sbt processes" the original was read from:

    gate: sbt exited 0, 3596 test results — GREEN

    okay.kafka.TestKafkaInterop        0.029s  (table said 237 s)
    okay.intent.TestCutStops           0.029s  (table said 39 s)
    okay.ui TestSwing, the law         1.658s  (table said timeout)
    okay.persist.TestStable            passed  (table said BindException)

The genuinely slow tests under that load are compute-bound and well
inside their budgets: 53.2 s "stack safety: 1M produced values",
37.5 s "does the gain survive resampling", 20.3 s "a typo's cost per
tier". None of the four named suites appears anywhere near them.

So box 1 needs no deadline and no `integrationTest` move. The three
real load-sensitive suites were found and fixed this morning by
another lane, with measurements, and the honest action here is to
correct the table rather than change four suites that were never
slow.

BOX 2, and the fix is kept while the diagnosis is not claimed.
`TestStable` asked the OS for a free port, CLOSED it, and handed the
number to `RaftWire.Node`, which binds it again. That is a real
TOCTOU by inspection, and this repo has met it before — `TestRaftWire`
and `TestRaftStore` both carry a documented three-try retry for
exactly it ("the BindException that failed a gate on 2026-09-06").
Those two must pre-bind, because their nodes are told each other's
ports before any of them starts. `TestStable` has no peers and never
reads its own port, so it can take the better road: port 0, the OS
assigns at bind time, and there is no window at all.

BUT I COULD NOT REPRODUCE THE COLLISION, in six models, and that is
recorded rather than buried:

    1. idiom + neighbours grabbing ephemeral ports    0 / 20 000
    2. 8 threads all running the idiom concurrently   0 / 32 000
    3. + 717 615 ports churned alongside              0 / 30 000
    4. gap 0/1/10/100/500 ms between close and bind   0 / 300 each
       (11.2 M ports churned during the 500 ms gaps)
    5. neighbours that HOLD what they take            0 / 300 each
    6. four separate JVMs racing the idiom at once    0 / 1 600

macOS does not appear to re-offer a just-released ephemeral port
while the cursor is anywhere near it, within a process or across
four. So the window is real in the code and very hard to hit on this
OS — which fits a failure seen twice in months, and means the change
here is "the step that buys nothing is gone", not "the flake is
fixed". The original gate log is gone, so I cannot check whether that
BindException was even this suite's; given the 237 s above, it is
worth doubting.
